course: dictionary
lesson: 7960
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is the context. «Maddie and Lily became best friends on the first day of Reception, because they both cried and the teacher sat them on the same squashy beanbag.» A teacher and a beanbag are clues that Reception here means a class at school.
explain: «Then he imagined playing football against the Reception class, scoring forty goals, and walking off feeling like a champion.» A class is a group of children, so Reception here means the youngest class of primary school. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it. Does «the first class of primary school» still make sense in «Ruby, Mia's best friend since Reception, was squashed onto the arm of the sofa»? It does, so that is the meaning. If the clues are not enough, a dictionary will settle it.
fact: Reception is a noun, and its plural is receptions.
fact: Reception can mean the desk or area where visitors are welcomed at a hotel, office, school or hospital.
fact: A reception can also be a formal party to welcome or celebrate, such as after a wedding.
fact: In England and Wales, Reception is the first class of primary school, for four- and five-year-olds.
fact: Reception can also mean how people react to something, as in «a warm reception», or how well a phone or radio receives signals, as in «no reception».
exercise: «Maddie and Lily became best friends on the first day of Reception.» What is Reception here? | A party to welcome guests | The desk at the entrance | A phone signal | *The first class of primary school
exercise: A sign says: Visitors, please sign in at reception. What does reception mean? | The first class of school | A wedding party | How people react to a play | *The desk where visitors are welcomed
exercise: Your phone shows «no reception» in the lift. What does reception mean? | How people react | *A phone signal | The youngest class at school | A welcome party
test: «The band had a warm reception from the crowd.» What does reception mean here? | A phone signal | *How people react to something | A class at school | A desk at a hotel
test: «After the wedding, there was a reception in the hall for all the guests.» What does reception mean here? | A place where visitors sign in | How well a radio works | The first class of school | *A formal party to celebrate
exam: «The Reception class was lining up for lunch.» Which meaning fits best? | How well a phone receives signals | A desk where visitors sign in | A party after a wedding | *The first class of primary school for four- and five-year-olds
